WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.601
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy Award-winning actress

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:06.701
you know from her iconic roles
on "Friends" and "The Comeback,"

00:00:06.701 --> 00:00:08.400
and films such as "Book Smart,"

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.868
"Romy and Michele's
High School Reunion"

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:11.334
and "Analyze This."

00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:14.000
She's the executive producer of
"Who Do You Think You Are,"

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.100
which airs Sundays on NBC and
streams the next day on Peacock.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.868
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.868 --> 00:00:21.767
-Wow. That's -- That is a --

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:23.934
That's a great-looking hat.

00:00:23.934 --> 00:00:26.200
And I am a hat lady.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:31.467
That looks like Pharrell
might have copied that hat.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:33.000
That looks like
it would fit my head.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.100
-[ Chuckles ]

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.868
If it was a little less
than 400 years old,

00:00:37.868 --> 00:00:39.334
we'd let you try it on.

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:41.467
-Please welcome to the show,
the one, the only

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:43.133
Lisa Kudrow, everybody!

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:45.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:55.133
♪♪

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:59.534
♪♪

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:01.467
-I am so happy
to have you here.

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:03.100
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.367
-I'm also happy to find that
we have something in common,

00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:06.868
especially during
these summer months

00:01:06.868 --> 00:01:08.400
when a lot of people
are drawn to it,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.434
but we both despise the beach.

00:01:10.434 --> 00:01:11.467
-Oh, yes.
-Yeah.

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:12.534
-Yeah, yeah.
No, I hate the sand.

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:14.601
-Have you hated it
your whole life?

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.133
-No, when I was a kid,
I didn't mind.

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:18.667
-Yeah.
-No, and then, I got smart.

00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:19.667
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:20.934
And I don't like it at all.

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:22.501
-The sand --
You really said it.

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:26.067
I think the sand is just --
I don't care how beautiful

00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:28.601
the ocean is,
the fact that it's right next

00:01:28.601 --> 00:01:30.501
to the sand
is just a deal killer.

00:01:30.501 --> 00:01:32.701
-No, the sand,
it gets everywhere,

00:01:32.701 --> 00:01:35.367
there are sand fleas maybe.
-Maybe.

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:37.767
I didn't even think about
the sand fleas element of this.

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:39.734
-Well, I just gave you
more evidence.

00:01:39.734 --> 00:01:42.901
-You know, the funny thing is,
my wife is the one who wants

00:01:42.901 --> 00:01:44.167
to go to the beach, and then,
we go to the beach,

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:45.534
and then we walk off the beach,
and she's like,

00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:47.434
"Will you brush the sand off
yourself before you get

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:49.834
in the car?" and I'm like,
"Or we don't go to the beach."

00:01:49.834 --> 00:01:51.100
-"Ever again!"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.334
-Yeah.
-I know.

00:01:52.334 --> 00:01:53.434
-And then, the car is pristine
the way the car

00:01:53.434 --> 00:01:54.934
is supposed to be.
-Right, exactly.

00:01:54.934 --> 00:01:56.868
My husband doesn't
like the beach, either,

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.467
but he scuba dives, snorkels,

00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:01.334
and that is out
of the question for me.

00:02:01.334 --> 00:02:03.067
-Is that worse to you
than being on sand?

00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:04.200
-Yes.
-Yeah, okay.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.267
-Yeah, yeah, no, because
I can't live underwater.

00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:08.968
[ Laughter ]
-True.

00:02:08.968 --> 00:02:12.300
-To me, my instincts tell me
I don't belong there,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.100
and then you put the snorkel on,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.667
and you see it's another world,
and I want out.

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:17.834
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:17.834 --> 00:02:19.634
-And by the way,
they want you out, too.

00:02:19.634 --> 00:02:22.534
Those beautiful fish
are not happy to see you.

00:02:22.534 --> 00:02:24.534
-Right! They're not there
for my entertainment.

00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:26.167
-Yeah.
-Yeah, I know.

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:28.467
-Yeah. If you had to,
would you rather scuba dive

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:29.968
or snorkel,
given the two options.

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:31.934
-Well, snorkel.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.934 --> 00:02:34.000
-Scuba dive, you have to learn
how to do it right

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.667
or you get seriously injured
or die. No.

00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:39.601
-I remember as a kid,
we went out to go scuba diving.

00:02:39.601 --> 00:02:41.100
I did it once with my family,
and, like, while they were

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.534
giving the instructions,
I was just like,

00:02:42.534 --> 00:02:44.000
"This isn't --
No, we gotta get out of here."

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.400
-It's what's called
an unnecessary risk.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.901
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.667
'Cause a lot of other people
do scuba diving,

00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:52.000
they take pictures
of all those fish.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.300
You can just look
at the pictures,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:54.834
and then you're good to go.
-There are so many shows.

00:02:54.834 --> 00:02:57.234
[ Laughter ]
-Speaking of shows --

00:02:57.234 --> 00:03:00.300
look at that --
you've got this wonderful show,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.267
and this --
so this was a show you saw

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:05.434
in the U.K. on BBC originally.
-Yeah, right.

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:07.133
-And did you immediately think,
"Oh, my God.

00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:10.234
I want to do that over here."
-Yes. I did.

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:12.100
-Were you drawn
to the idea of genealogy?

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.734
Was this something that was
always interesting to you?

00:03:13.734 --> 00:03:19.534
-No, not even a little bit.
But this show had almost --

00:03:19.534 --> 00:03:22.534
Genealogy was just the vehicle
that got you to these

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:26.968
phenomenal stories about
ancestry and about our history,

00:03:26.968 --> 00:03:28.501
and it's our shared history.

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:31.701
And I felt watching it, like,
we're all connected.

00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:34.501
Like, when we hear these
stories, I know about that,

00:03:34.501 --> 00:03:36.734
and, look how it affected
your family,

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:39.634
what affected my family.
Yeah, and I just thought,

00:03:39.634 --> 00:03:41.234
"Why don't --"
I actually just was

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:44.000
kind of angry --
"Why don't we have this show?

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.067
What?
We're stupid? No, we're not."

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:47.734
[ Laughter ]
You know, like,

00:03:47.734 --> 00:03:50.000
"We would, like,
totally watch this show!"

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.300
[ Laughter ]
-Well, thank you for

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.701
standing up for us.

00:03:52.701 --> 00:03:54.734
On behalf of all Americans,
I thank you.

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.334
-In a baby, baby way.

00:03:56.334 --> 00:03:59.267
-Did you know a lot about
your genealogy growing up?

00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:01.667
Was your family the kind that
shared that sort of thing?

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:05.167
-My dad did.
He would go to the Mormon temple

00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:07.467
and do research and all of that.

00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:10.467
But it was just like
names and dates and okay.

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:14.100
So, "Oh, Feivel, Zelig, okay.
That's interesting,"

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.601
but you don't know
anything about them.

00:04:15.601 --> 00:04:19.667
But this show made it seem like,
"Oh, but you can maybe --

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:21.334
There are records maybe
about people,

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:22.834
and you can learn something
about them."

00:04:22.834 --> 00:04:25.567
So I got in touch with the guy
who created the show,

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:28.100
and he said, "Yeah, we've been
trying to do it in the U.S.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.868
for a long time.
Let's try it together,"

00:04:30.868 --> 00:04:33.434
and NBC said yes, and --

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:36.067
Oh, God.
I had to do the first episode.

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:37.701
-And did you
find out anything --

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:40.000
Were you nervous?
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.300
-I've done this once.
I did one of the other shows.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.934
-Yeah, I watched it.
I love that show, yeah.

00:04:44.934 --> 00:04:48.033
-But I wasn't nervous --
'Cause I feel like there's

00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:49.801
two kinds of nervous
you can be with these shows.

00:04:49.801 --> 00:04:51.834
One, I was nervous
mine was gonna be boring.

00:04:51.834 --> 00:04:53.534
-Right.
-The other one is that

00:04:53.534 --> 00:04:56.434
you find out some bad things.
-Oh, horrible things.

00:04:56.434 --> 00:04:59.667
Yeah, but most people --
I mean, look, for our show,

00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:03.501
I think what makes people
nervous is they're actually

00:05:03.501 --> 00:05:07.434
going to the places
where their ancestors were,

00:05:07.434 --> 00:05:10.968
and they know that they're
interacting with historians,

00:05:10.968 --> 00:05:14.801
and people, I think,
are really nervous that they're

00:05:14.801 --> 00:05:17.400
going to look dumb
because they don't remember

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.167
details of history that --
I'm sorry -- were not taught,

00:05:20.167 --> 00:05:24.200
and, yeah, we don't remember
Mayflower landed in 1620,

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.601
which I only know
because we've had 10 people --

00:05:28.601 --> 00:05:31.133
you know, I mean,
I've seen it 10 times.

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:34.000
But, yeah, no, you're not meant
to remember all that stuff.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.400
-Do people come to you now
and say they want to do it?

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.901
-Yeah.
-Okay, so people reach out.

00:05:37.901 --> 00:05:39.300
-Yeah, they do.
-Because it really is --

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:42.234
I mean, the way this show works,
which is, as you mentioned,

00:05:42.234 --> 00:05:44.234
people travel, and they get
to go to these places

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:46.767
they would never go,
and I imagine, some people,

00:05:46.767 --> 00:05:48.868
that must be
so magnetic an idea.

00:05:48.868 --> 00:05:51.067
-Yeah, I think that is
exciting to them,

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:53.767
but I think they're also,
they just want to --

00:05:53.767 --> 00:05:54.901
'Cause you know you'll be

00:05:54.901 --> 00:05:57.767
walking in your
ancestor's footsteps.

00:05:57.767 --> 00:06:00.267
There's just something about
being right outside the building

00:06:00.267 --> 00:06:02.534
where,
"You mean this is where...?

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:04.567
I'm in the same spot."
-Yeah.

00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:08.200
-"These are the same bricks
that he touched,"

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.234
and usually it's a "he."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:13.501
-Well, a lot of countries, they
wouldn't let women touch bricks.

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:15.033
-No, that's right.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:17.467
See? You know more than me.
Thank you! No.

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:20.934
-I know that,
I know Mayflower's 1620.

00:06:20.934 --> 00:06:24.067
-Until five minutes from now.
[ Both laugh ]

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:27.567
-I want to ask this, as well,
because the pandemic was a time

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:29.467
where so many people went
and watched old shows.

00:06:29.467 --> 00:06:31.567
And I'm wondering
if someone like you --

00:06:31.567 --> 00:06:34.300
I mean, with that said,
it strikes me that "Friends"

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.601
was a show that people did not
need a pandemic

00:06:36.601 --> 00:06:38.167
to go back and watch.
-Yeah.

00:06:38.167 --> 00:06:40.767
-Have you found new fans over --

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:42.701
with shows like "Friends"
or "The Comeback" --

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:44.767
-Yes.
-That's great.

00:06:44.767 --> 00:06:47.367
-Yes, I mean,
"Friends" is not shocking.

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:49.534
It was when it was on Netflix
and all of a sudden,

00:06:49.534 --> 00:06:51.567
there are all these 8-year-olds
who weren't born, saying,

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:56.534
"Oh, my God, are you Phoebe?"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.534 --> 00:06:59.634
But, no, "The Comeback,"
and then, there's these

00:06:59.634 --> 00:07:02.634
young people who are like, "Oh,
God, I love 'The Comeback,'"

00:07:02.634 --> 00:07:06.033
like, "What?! You do?!
Oh, my God."

00:07:06.033 --> 00:07:08.934
But the most thrilling thing --
I just have to say this --

00:07:08.934 --> 00:07:13.000
is my son had COVID,
and so he was stuck,

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.033
and called me up and said,
"So I just watched, like,

00:07:16.033 --> 00:07:18.534
episode -- like,
the first two episode.

00:07:18.534 --> 00:07:21.400
It's really good, Mom."
I said, "Thanks."

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:25.167
He said, "Can I ask you
some questions about that?"

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:27.667
Like, "Yes!"
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:30.667
I almost started crying.
-That's really great.

00:07:30.667 --> 00:07:32.200
-I didn't think that, you know,

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.901
anyone in my family
liked that show.

00:07:34.901 --> 00:07:36.234
[ Laughter ]

00:07:36.234 --> 00:07:38.334
-Did you --
So you obviously knew

00:07:38.334 --> 00:07:41.200
at the time
your son did not watch it.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.334
-Well, no, he was 5, 6, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:07:43.334 --> 00:07:44.601
Well, that's --
they say "The Comeback"

00:07:44.601 --> 00:07:47.734
is for 7 and above.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:47.734 --> 00:07:49.434
What is his relationship
with "Friends"?

00:07:49.434 --> 00:07:51.267
Is that a show
that he's watched?

00:07:51.267 --> 00:07:54.033
I mean, obviously, it was --
you know, he came after, but --

00:07:54.033 --> 00:07:56.634
-Right, right. No, he was 5
when we were finished.

00:07:56.634 --> 00:07:58.667
-Gotcha.
-So, but still...

00:07:58.667 --> 00:08:02.000
No, no, he did start watching
when his friends at school

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.133
were watching, and you got to
know what everyone's watching.

00:08:04.133 --> 00:08:06.601
-Yeah.
-So he watched it,

00:08:06.601 --> 00:08:07.968
and, no, he was impressed.

00:08:07.968 --> 00:08:11.534
He said -- He goes,
"It's actually really funny."

00:08:11.534 --> 00:08:13.067
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.067 --> 00:08:15.734
This little kid, he's like...
-He said, "Actually"?

00:08:15.734 --> 00:08:17.567
-Kind of, yeah. He said,
"It's actually really funny.

00:08:17.567 --> 00:08:20.100
And, I mean,
the guys are so funny."

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.801
[ Laughter ]

00:08:22.801 --> 00:08:26.701
And he's like,
"No, I mean, you're funny, too."

00:08:26.701 --> 00:08:31.100
And I never in my life have I
wanted to tell my own kid,

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:34.267
like, "[Bleep] you."
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:34.267 --> 00:08:35.801
I mean, I thought it.
-I didn't say it.

00:08:35.801 --> 00:08:37.167
-You thought it.
You didn't say it.

00:08:37.167 --> 00:08:40.501
-No, no, I didn't say it.
-By the way, I think, you know,

00:08:40.501 --> 00:08:43.501
for all parents, you think it.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:43.501 --> 00:08:45.534
The difference between
a good parent and a bad parent

00:08:45.534 --> 00:08:47.434
is, like, "Do you say it?"
Yeah.

00:08:47.434 --> 00:08:50.834
-I mean, listen, it's not
required that you're a fan.

00:08:50.834 --> 00:08:56.968
And you don't have to like what
I do, but don't be so demeaning.

00:08:56.968 --> 00:08:58.067
[ Laughter ]

00:08:58.067 --> 00:09:00.434
It's so wow.

00:09:00.434 --> 00:09:02.601
-You were the voice
of an animated poodle.

00:09:02.601 --> 00:09:04.667
-Yeah, yeah, Honey.
-This is very exciting.

00:09:04.667 --> 00:09:06.434
-Yeah, her name's Honey.
-Honey.

00:09:06.434 --> 00:09:09.234
You have a real -- is this half
a poodle or a full poodle?

00:09:09.234 --> 00:09:11.267
-She's like a quarter poodle.
-A quarter poodle.

00:09:11.267 --> 00:09:13.968
-She's really cute, and she's
more elegant than that.

00:09:13.968 --> 00:09:16.834
-Oh, do you -- did you use her
as any inspiration

00:09:16.834 --> 00:09:18.868
for the voice
of the fictional poodle?

00:09:18.868 --> 00:09:20.601
-Yeah, I kind of tried.
-Yeah.

00:09:20.601 --> 00:09:23.434
-You know, and then, it just
turned out to be me, but...

00:09:23.434 --> 00:09:24.968
[ Laughter ]

00:09:24.968 --> 00:09:28.667
But, no, I mean, I tried.
-Do you --

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:30.334
You and your husband
both hate the beach.

00:09:30.334 --> 00:09:32.534
Do you both
love the dog equally?

00:09:32.534 --> 00:09:36.133
-Uh, yes -- no,
my husband loves her more.

00:09:36.133 --> 00:09:37.667
My husband actually --

00:09:37.667 --> 00:09:40.067
This will sound weird,
but it's true.

00:09:40.067 --> 00:09:42.667
He says all the time,
"I just want to take --

00:09:42.667 --> 00:09:45.100
I want to take her to Paris."
He's French.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:47.400
So he says, "I want to take
her to Paris

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.501
and have dinner with her
at the Eiffel Tower."

00:09:49.501 --> 00:09:51.801
[ Laughter ]

00:09:51.801 --> 00:09:53.601
What?

00:09:53.601 --> 00:09:55.367
-No, that doesn't sound weird.

00:09:55.367 --> 00:09:58.267
[ Laughter ]

00:09:58.267 --> 00:10:01.367
-"No, I love her so much,
and it's such a pretty view."

00:10:01.367 --> 00:10:03.868
-Do they even let you bring dogs
to the Eiffel Tower?

00:10:03.868 --> 00:10:05.767
I bet, being Paris,
they probably do, right?

00:10:05.767 --> 00:10:07.334
-If they're small.

00:10:07.334 --> 00:10:08.801
Don't they bring their dogs
everywhere in Paris?

00:10:08.801 --> 00:10:10.033
-Yeah, maybe they do.

00:10:10.033 --> 00:10:11.901
You're the one who's married
to the French guy.

00:10:11.901 --> 00:10:13.534
-I know, but I don't know
everything about it.

00:10:13.534 --> 00:10:14.834
[ Laughter ]

00:10:14.834 --> 00:10:16.367
-You're too busy learning
about the genealogy

00:10:16.367 --> 00:10:17.400
of other celebrities.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:18.467
You don't have time
for your husband.

00:10:18.467 --> 00:10:20.567
-That's right, you see.

00:10:20.567 --> 00:10:21.934
-Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:10:21.934 --> 00:10:23.234
It's such a delight
to talk to you.

00:10:23.234 --> 00:10:24.400
Congratulations on the show.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:25.901
I can't wait to watch
the new season.

00:10:25.901 --> 00:10:27.200
Lisa Kudrow, everybody.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:30.000
"Who Do You Think You Are?"
airs Sundays at 7:00 p.m.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.067
on NBC and streams
the next day on Peacock.

00:10:32.067 --> 00:10:33.968
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

